WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:07.440
I made it? I actually made it? I’m still alive,&nbsp;
by some miracle – or maybe by sheer luck – I made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.240
it to the end of my sentence. Over three months&nbsp;
of the most horrific work I’ve ever had to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.580
with no other alternative. But it’s paid off,&nbsp;
because any minute now, they’ll be coming into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.140
dorm to let me go. I can walk out of here with my&nbsp;
head up. I can’t wait to get out of these orange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:28.140
overalls that I’ve had to live in since I arrived.
It seems like so much longer, in all honesty. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.560
only way I’ve been able to keep track of how&nbsp;
many days I’ve been stuck here is by putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.440
tally marks on the wall next to my dormitory&nbsp;
bed. A hundred, I’ve been a member of D-Class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.540
personnel for the SCP Foundation for the last&nbsp;
one hundred days. And here’s how I survived…&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.220
They didn’t waste any time early on; I’ll give&nbsp;
the Foundation that. I’d barely been out of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.960
sentencing hearing for a few days. Put on trial&nbsp;
and convicted for a crime I didn’t even commit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.860
There had been a string of murders, and someone&nbsp;
who had either been involved or was responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:01.140
laid the blame on me. But whoever they were, they&nbsp;
had powerful friends. The cops were in on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.700
planting evidence to frame me and make it look&nbsp;
like I was guilty when I was really innocent.&nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.220
That’s what landed me in prison, serving a&nbsp;
sentence of twenty-five years to life behind bars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.560
And it was during my first week of incarceration&nbsp;
that he showed up. The recruitment specialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.420
a clean-cut, blunt, Agent Smith type came&nbsp;
to visit me. I hadn’t even been in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.020
long enough to have visitor privileges, but&nbsp;
the shady agent seemed to know which strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:30.660
to pull in order to ask me an important question:
“What would you do to get out of here?” he’d said.&nbsp;

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.980
To which my answer was “Anything.”
That was my first mistake.&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.540
Immediately, the next day I received a letter&nbsp;
detailing more about what exactly Agent Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.520
was offering. It was folded and hidden in the&nbsp;
spine of a book that a guard handed me during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.560
reading hour. Unfolding the message, it all&nbsp;
felt like an old-school covert spy tactic.&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.080
“To Mr Emil Carker,” the letter read. “We&nbsp;
understand that you recently received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.120
criminal conviction. There are two options&nbsp;
currently before you, as detailed below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.160
One: you can serve out the remainder of your&nbsp;
sentence in prison. Or two: you can be released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.840
into the care of our organization. While with&nbsp;
us, you will be helping to further scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.180
advancements through hard work. We have devised&nbsp;
a system that allows convicts such as yourself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:21.120
perform a vital role within our organization, in&nbsp;
return for a reduction in their prison sentences.&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.620
“We have no interest in or intention of&nbsp;
determining any guilt for the crimes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:31.620
which you are currently convicted. We merely seek&nbsp;
to present this opportunity to you. We ask that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.000
you destroy this letter once you have finished&nbsp;
reading. Should you be interested in our offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.780
then please recite the phrase ‘it’s a yes’ to&nbsp;
a prison guard with the badge number: 47890.”&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.280
Naturally, as cryptic as an offer like that&nbsp;
was, I didn’t need much time to mull it over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.060
I was trapped in prison for something I&nbsp;
didn’t do, with no way to appeal for my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.040
freedom or prove my innocence. So, taking up&nbsp;
a mysterious job opportunity from a shadowy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.900
group seemed like a much better alternative at&nbsp;
the time. After all, I could either work off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.820
my sentence through employment, or rot away in a&nbsp;
cell for the next two and half decades at minimum.&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.920
All I had to do next was find the right guard.&nbsp;
I tried not to make it obvious that I was eyeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.000
every prison guard’s badge number as they&nbsp;
stood around, keeping a close eye on me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.060
and the other inmates. But it was while out&nbsp;
in the yard that I saw him: number 47890.&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.740
I had gone to bed with a knot in my stomach.&nbsp;
Hours earlier, I had done exactly as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.280
letter said and approached the guard, making&nbsp;
sure I was close enough for him to hear me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.240
but trying to make it clear that I wasn’t&nbsp;
looking tor start any trouble either.&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.880
“It’s a yes,” I told him.
In response, 47890 had furrowed his brow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.740
scrunched his mustache, seemingly in disgust.
“Back up, inmate,” he commanded sharply.&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.000
I backed away with my hands raised, confused as&nbsp;
to what had just happened. I spent the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.620
rest of the day thinking about it; maybe the&nbsp;
note had been a prank, some kind of initiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.760
seeing as I was the newest prisoner. But that&nbsp;
didn’t explain the agent who had shown up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.740
before. What was going on? The questions that were&nbsp;
spinning around in my head eventually wore me out.&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.220
And while I slept, that’s when&nbsp;
someone put a bag over my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.620
drugged me, and snuck me out of prison.
When I woke up, I was somewhere new, a room filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.700
with other convicts. Although not many of them&nbsp;
seemed to have come from the same prison I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.440
been sentenced to; plenty were sporting different&nbsp;
jumpsuits, but their gruff demeanors told me all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.880
I needed to about them. I was surrounded by&nbsp;
a more violent breed of criminal, some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.680
worst of the absolute worst. The atmosphere in&nbsp;
this pen full of murderers and monsters was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.060
tense that it felt like the slightest accidental&nbsp;
bump could explode into a full-blown fight.&nbsp;

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.100
Suddenly, a hatch in the ceiling opened, and a&nbsp;
pile of clothes came thumping down from above.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:43.380
It was a mass of orange, enough matching overalls&nbsp;
for every inmate in the room. Of course, I held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.580
back from the initial clamor some of the others&nbsp;
made, grabbing their new prison garb, snatching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.560
orange overalls from each other, and arguing. When&nbsp;
I eventually got mine, I noticed an unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.120
logo emblazoned on the front of the uniform.
After being made to wait a whole day in the pen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.440
the other prisoners and I were filed out of&nbsp;
the room by heavily armed security guards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.520
We were all directed towards a hall, nobody&nbsp;
was daring enough to challenge the officers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.780
even the more violent among the prisoners.&nbsp;
I spotted that the guard’s uniforms bore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.160
same insignia as the overalls we’d been made to&nbsp;
wear. It must’ve been the logo of the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.720
organization that had offered me employment.
We were given an orientation talk led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.400
someone who introduced themselves as a Junior&nbsp;
Assistant Researcher. They explained that our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.840
current location was highly classified, as was&nbsp;
the true identity of the group that had arranged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.900
for us to be released. All we were told was that&nbsp;
this was an unspecified form of research facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.060
and that we had to cooperate with the facility’s&nbsp;
staff if we wanted to secure our release. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.540
seemed straightforward enough, although&nbsp;
the Junior Assistant Researcher seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:46.680
make a lot of jokes about us dying during&nbsp;
these tests. I think they hoped it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.300
alleviate some of the tension… it didn’t.
After sitting through the orientation talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:56.460
I still had more questions than answers. The&nbsp;
one thing I had learned was that being tattooed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.620
hurt. Exiting the hall, the other prisoners and&nbsp;
I had been directed to get a designation number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.060
tattooed on our wrist and across our chest.&nbsp;
When someone had asked why the chest, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.920
Researcher conducting orientation had answered:
“Well, in the event of an explosion, it's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.960
likely that it'll be the largest intact chunk of&nbsp;
meat left.” This was another of their ‘jokes’.&nbsp;

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.240
Still feeling sore as my tattoo healed, I read&nbsp;
the number now permanently inked onto my body:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.320
D-2152. ‘D-Class Personnel’ that’s what they&nbsp;
called us now. We were directed into a dormitory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.700
a lot less cramped than my old cell. But&nbsp;
still, being surrounded by violent criminals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.020
when I knew I didn’t belong there, it&nbsp;
felt no less isolating. One of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.000
who took the bunk next to mine, introduced himself&nbsp;
to me as ‘Shiv’. Worried he might try to kill me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.340
I did my best to be friendly towards him.
Shiv and I didn’t exactly bond, but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:52.320
sprung up conversations because there was little&nbsp;
else to do. I was still worried about some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.300
flippant comments made about us all dying,&nbsp;
until he pointed out something far stranger.&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.020
“Don’t you wonder why they’re&nbsp;
making us wait?” he asked.&nbsp;

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.700
And the moment he mentioned it, I did start&nbsp;
to wonder about that. It seemed odd that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.880
organization had wasted little time&nbsp;
in getting us all here. It had only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.780
been a week ago that I entered my prison&nbsp;
cell for the first time, now I was here.&nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.580
“I think it’s their way of telling us,” Shiv&nbsp;
went on. “That we aren’t here to be helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.880
to be a workforce for them. They’ve&nbsp;
brought us all here to die.”&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.220
That comment made me look around, and actually&nbsp;
acknowledge the people I was trapped with. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:30.960
were some of the worst criminals imaginable,&nbsp;
irredeemable killers. Would this organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.740
really let those people back out into the&nbsp;
world after helping out with a few tests?&nbsp;

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:38.820
I’d soon find out, because Day 8&nbsp;
was the last day before I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:44.160
myself working directly for the SCP Foundation.
I picked up the name from a few places around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.680
facility; nametags, lab coats, and security&nbsp;
uniforms, always with the same logo. Me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.300
the other D-Class personnel were woken up early in&nbsp;
the morning by a bell and issued with assignments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.040
From the very first day the work started, I&nbsp;
noticed that not all the others came back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.140
dorm at the end of the day. Shiv was one of those.
My tasks mostly consisted of cleaning up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:09.240
mop, hosing down empty testing chambers that had&nbsp;
a worrying amount of blood sprayed over the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:14.280
and floors. The regiments were strict, and I made&nbsp;
sure to do exactly what was asked of me to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.980
causing any trouble. It felt a lot like being back&nbsp;
in prison, but with the only added caveat of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.840
able to move around the facility, even if it&nbsp;
was to go and wipe up the Foundation’s mess.&nbsp;

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:30.180
Although the other classes of personnel didn’t&nbsp;
seem to take kindly to my help, making snide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:34.320
comments as they passed me in the hallways. We had&nbsp;
been told not to address the other staff unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:39.120
spoken to, which was frustrating. I certainly&nbsp;
had a few things I would’ve liked to say to them.&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.800
After two and a half weeks without so much as a&nbsp;
hint of any trouble, I was given my first testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.820
assignment that wasn’t just cleaning up. You&nbsp;
would think that would be exciting, but having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:53.160
seen some of the carnage left behind after other&nbsp;
tests, I was anything but eager to take part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.080
Luckily for me, and pretty surprisingly,&nbsp;
my first test was pretty straightforward.&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.220
I was shown to a room containing a&nbsp;
white bowl decorated with light blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.860
flowers that the researchers referred to&nbsp;
as SCP-348. I had received a nasty splinter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.120
from my mop’s handle the day before, and was&nbsp;
instructed to sit down and eat from the bowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:17.700
which was filled with soup. Naturally, I eagerly&nbsp;
ate it all, despite how bitter it tasted. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.200
better than the food from the D-Class canteen at&nbsp;
least. But when I finished, I noticed a message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:28.320
had appeared at the bottom of SCP-348. It read:
“I don’t believe you were framed. Goodbye, son.”&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.880
After that, it was as if the floodgates had&nbsp;
been opened, and every day I was asked to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:38.160
another test with a new SCP. Most were harmless&nbsp;
anomalous creatures or objects, like one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.980
bigger experiments I was involved in, which&nbsp;
focused on SCP-999, this friendly gelatinous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.260
orange blob. Electrodes were hooked up to me&nbsp;
while I just sat calmly in a room with SCP-999.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:55.080
Being around it made me feel great, the best I had&nbsp;
felt since arriving and joining D-Class. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:01.260
I did hear one of the researchers making the&nbsp;
comment that SCP-999 was ‘not quite ready yet’.&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.960
Even so, the Tickle Monster had given me a new&nbsp;
positive outlook on my role at the Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.180
It was odd work, but I could find a rhythm to&nbsp;
it that would hopefully make the time pass by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.800
a little quicker. And if it meant getting out, it&nbsp;
was worth all the dirty looks from other classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:22.140
of personnel. This was an outlook I didn’t want&nbsp;
to lose. After a month, we were told to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:26.940
pill that the guards handed out to us, something&nbsp;
called an amnestic that would make us all forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:32.160
the previous month. But, wanting to hang on to my&nbsp;
newfound optimism, I secretly flushed mine down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.380
a toilet. In hindsight, I should have taken it.
My next big test quickly brought me back down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:44.400
peg. I was handed a katana, SCP-572, and instantly&nbsp;
felt like I was unstoppable. The sword made me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.720
believe I was a powerful warrior, as if I could&nbsp;
maybe fight my way out of the Foundation facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.000
with ease. Unfortunately, the moment I tried to&nbsp;
use it didn’t lead to freedom. I had an accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.100
broke my arm and suffered a series of internal&nbsp;
fractures, all while the research team mocked me.&nbsp;

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:05.520
Embarrassed, I felt like a total laughing stock.
Things only got worse from there. I had barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.240
set foot outside of the infirmary before a&nbsp;
different team of personnel told me I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:13.200
going to be assisting them with an important&nbsp;
task. They dragged me to an old wooden door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.180
after briefly explaining what was going on;&nbsp;
although I struggled to keep up. Beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:24.240
door – which they called SCP-2317 – was a&nbsp;
vast salt pan. Following their instructions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:30.060
I acted as an assistant to the rest of the team as&nbsp;
they performed a strange ritual. I had to scatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:35.280
a mix of holy water and chicken blood around&nbsp;
a circle of seven stone pillars, then recite:&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:41.760
“Blood for the Old Gods, Water for the New King.”
It was an unusual practice, to say the least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:45.600
but apparently, I was helping keep a&nbsp;
powerful, vengeful demigod imprisoned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.700
That did little to soften the news that I&nbsp;
heard when we returned through SCP-2317.&nbsp;

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:54.840
While I was gone, one of the Foundation’s&nbsp;
security team had been infected by something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:00.900
known as SCP-2193, a phenomenon that makes&nbsp;
people believe that every month a large group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:07.020
of D-Class are to be ‘terminated’. When I heard,&nbsp;
I actually considered taking my amnestic for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:11.880
month to forget just how many had been killed…
It had been a whole two months since I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:16.020
actively started work as part of D-Class&nbsp;
Personnel, and there weren’t many of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:21.120
fellow prisoners left. I was sent for a mandatory&nbsp;
psychological evaluation; the Foundation wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.220
to know if I would still be able to perform tests&nbsp;
for them. I doubted it came from a place of actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:30.960
concern, it’s not like they cared all that much&nbsp;
if the things I had seen were taking a toll on me.&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:36.000
Sitting across from the researcher performing my&nbsp;
evaluation, I did my best impression of a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.800
person. I had to pretend like none of it was&nbsp;
getting to me, all the horrors and the close brush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:45.480
I’d almost had with death by Monthly Termination.&nbsp;
I wouldn’t have lied about it normally, But I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.400
been told I had a month left before I was due to&nbsp;
be released. Apparently, not many D-Class made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:55.200
that far, given the dangerous nature of testing.&nbsp;
All I could think about was how good it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:00.120
feel to eat real food again; I started to daydream&nbsp;
about getting a cheeseburger on my first day out.&nbsp;

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:05.340
Almost instantly, my hopes for freedom were&nbsp;
dashed the very next day. I was told I’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:10.440
going on a longer assignment, this time it would&nbsp;
be an expedition into an anomalous location called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:17.340
SCP-432. The Foundation’s researchers outfitted&nbsp;
me with a flashlight, plus extra batteries. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.840
headset and microphone linked to their control&nbsp;
center were placed over my ear, along with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:26.160
camera unit mounted on my shoulder; they said&nbsp;
it would wirelessly transmit back to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:30.900
and they’d see what I saw. I was also given a&nbsp;
couple of bottles of water, some energy bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:35.820
and a few sticks of luminous marker chalk.
But when they had said ‘anomalous location’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:40.080
the last thing I’d been expecting was&nbsp;
to climb into a rusty metal cabinet.&nbsp;

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:45.720
The next two weeks were like living inside a&nbsp;
nightmare. Inside the cabinet was a huge maze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:52.020
a literal labyrinth all made out of the same&nbsp;
rusted steel of the exterior of SCP-432. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:56.220
so dark, even with the use of my flashlight and&nbsp;
the few lightbulbs affixed to some of the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:01.320
within the Cabinet Maze. Before long, I ended&nbsp;
up losing my bearings, lost with no way out.&nbsp;

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:05.040
But being trapped in the maze and the&nbsp;
dark wasn’t even the worst parts. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.060
was something else in the labyrinth, some&nbsp;
kind of creature lurking through the metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:13.800
corridors. My flashlight eventually ran&nbsp;
out of power, so I never actually saw it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:18.540
but every time I tried to sleep, I’d just lay&nbsp;
awake, listening to its growls. I couldn’t help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:23.640
what kind of horrible thing was out there, and&nbsp;
might be looming over me in the dark. Even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:29.520
I opened my eyes, I still wouldn’t get to see it.
Eventually, another D-Class was sent into SCP-432.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:33.960
By some miracle, he was able to not only find&nbsp;
me, but guide me back to the entrance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:39.600
Cabinet Maze. The moment I was free, I yelled at&nbsp;
the research team, demanding they let me go. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:45.480
didn’t just mean let out of SCP-432, I meant free&nbsp;
from D-Class, from the Foundation, from all of it.&nbsp;

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:49.740
I was sent for another psyche evaluation&nbsp;
and deemed to be fine, just suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:54.540
heightened stress. When they asked me if I’d been&nbsp;
taking my amnestics, I lied and told them yes.&nbsp;

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.280
Ten days before I was set to be released and they&nbsp;
couldn’t help but assign me to one last test.&nbsp;

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:05.460
“Don’t worry,” a researcher sarcastically assured&nbsp;
me. “This one will be nice and easy on you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:10.020
you’ll just have to jump into a paddling&nbsp;
pool. You can do that, can’t you, D-2152?”&nbsp;

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:15.660
The paddling pool in question was SCP-120.&nbsp;
Every time a different glow emanated from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:20.940
I was instructed to jump in. The exact second I&nbsp;
did, I found myself transported somewhere new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:25.800
I got The Himalayas on my first go, Greenland&nbsp;
and the Sahara Desert on the next two tries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:29.700
Every one of the locations I ended up&nbsp;
at had some kind of Foundation facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:35.580
established nearby. They’d pick me up, and I’d&nbsp;
be taken back to SCP-120 to continue testing.&nbsp;

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:40.260
It felt like I was being deliberately,&nbsp;
intentionally tortured. The Foundation was giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:45.960
me these momentary glimpses of freedom every time&nbsp;
I was teleported by SCP-120. It was a carrot on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:50.040
the end of a stick that they were keeping just&nbsp;
out of my reach, and I hated it. It made me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.600
realize that I had never been there to work for&nbsp;
them, being part of D-Class Personnel wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:00.780
a job. All I was to them was a human lab rat.
Now it’s my last day. They’ve promised me that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:06.540
the end of today, I won’t be here anymore. I just&nbsp;
want to be out, done with D-Class for good. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:11.280
Foundation’s researchers have given me one last,&nbsp;
simple test to do before I go. They said if I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:17.100
then I’ll be gone. All they want me to do is wear&nbsp;
this amulet for someone called Doctor Bright. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:21.180
hard can that be? And they said they’d let me&nbsp;
go after this one last thing, so it can’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:26.220
anything all that bad, right? Right…?
